+/* PORT (patch 0024): the console build described the Game Boy sound registers NR10..NR52 (mirrored in D_8120EA70, indexed by
+ * register address - 0xFF10) as a struct of C bit-fields (GbApuRegs, 0x18 bytes) and read them as GB_APU_REGS.<field>. The
+ * MIPS compiler places the FIRST declared bit-field in the MOST significant bits of a byte; the x86 compilers used for the
+ * native build place it in the LEAST significant bits. So natively every field read the wrong bits (for example "power", meant to
+ * be bit 7 of NR52, read bit 0), the emulator thought the sound hardware was switched off and ignored every register write:
+ * the Game Boy Tower produced silence.
+ * The accessors below take each field from the byte it lives in with an explicit shift and mask. The positions are the
+ * MIPS o32 big-endian layout of the original struct as computed by clang (--target=mips-linux-gnu -fdump-record-layouts-simple)
+ * and agree with the documented Game Boy register layout (Pan Docs: NR10 = bits 6-4 sweep time, bit 3 direction, bits 2-0 shift;
+ * NR11 = bits 7-6 duty, bits 5-0 length; NR12 = bits 7-4 volume, bit 3 direction, bits 2-0 period; NR14 = bit 7 trigger, bit 6
+ * length enable, bits 2-0 frequency high; NR43 = bits 7-4 clock shift, bit 3 width, bits 2-0 divisor; NR50 = bit 7 VIN left,
+ * bits 6-4 left volume, bit 3 VIN right, bits 2-0 right volume; NR51 = one bit per channel, noise/wave/sq2/sq1 left then right;
+ * NR52 = bit 7 power). Every use in this file is a READ, so only reading macros are needed.
+ * GB_APU_BITS(byte, shift, mask) = (D_8120EA70[byte] >> shift) & mask. The fields (byte = offset in D_8120EA70) are: */
